---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import StructuredOutputValidator from '../../components/StructuredOutputValidator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="AI Structured Output Validator" description="Check whether an AI model's JSON output matches your expected schema — required fields and types.">
  <StructuredOutputValidator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why validate AI JSON output</h2>
  <p>Even with function calling or JSON mode, models occasionally return malformed or incomplete structured output. Validating against your expected schema before using the data in production catches these cases early.</p>

  <h2>Common Structured Output Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trusting AI-generated JSON without validation</strong>, even when using structured output features that reduce but don't eliminate malformed responses entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Not handling validation failures gracefully</strong>, causing a hard crash instead of a retry or fallback when output doesn't match the expected schema.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Structured Output Validator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Validating AI-generated JSON before using it in downstream application logic</li>
    <li>Debugging why a model's structured output isn't matching your expected schema</li>
    <li>Testing schema compliance during development of a new AI feature</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Structured Output Validation FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this work with any JSON schema?</strong> It checks required fields and basic types against what you define — for full JSON Schema features like nested validation or regex patterns, a dedicated schema validator library is more appropriate.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I validate output even when using a provider's JSON mode?</strong> Yes — JSON mode guarantees syntactically valid JSON, not that it matches your expected schema's fields and types, so validation is still worthwhile.</p>
</ToolLayout>
